@code{cmacs-calculator-families} lists the family features the landing page
loads.  Note it names @code{cmacs-calculator-tax-data}, not
@code{cmacs-calculator-tax}: loading the data file pulls in the math @emph{and}
registers the jurisdiction tables, without which the tax calculators have no
rates to work from.

@subsection Evaluation API

@table @code
@item (cmacs-calculator-eval EXPR &optional MODES)
Result @emph{string}, numerically evaluated with corrected semantics.  Signals
on bad input.
@item (cmacs-calculator-eval-symbolic EXPR &optional MODES)
Result string, free variables permitted, not forced to a number.
@item (cmacs-calculator-eval-number EXPR &optional MODES)
Emacs @emph{float}, or nil if the result is not a plain real number (infinite,
complex, symbolic).
@item (cmacs-calculator-eval-symbolic-number EXPR &optional MODES)
Float or nil, permitting free variables --- for callers that substitute
variables themselves, notably plotting.
@item (cmacs-calculator-to-base-units EXPR &optional MODES)
Result string in base SI units.
@item (cmacs-calculator-convert-units EXPR UNITS &optional MODES)
Result string converted to @var{UNITS}.
@item (cmacs-calculator-supported-p)
Non-nil when the engine is usable.
@end table

@var{MODES} is a flat list of Calc @var{VARIABLE VALUE} overrides spliced into
@code{calc-eval}'s list form --- the only mechanism Calc honours
(@pxref{Calculator Semantics}).

Errors are all subtypes of @code{cmacs-calculator-error}, so one handler catches
everything: @code{cmacs-calculator-syntax-error},
@code{cmacs-calculator-unknown-function},
@code{cmacs-calculator-unbound-variable}.

@subsection Defining a Calculator

@code{cmacs-calculator-defcalc} defines the function with Calc's @code{defmath}
(so it is a first-class algebraic function) and registers its metadata:

@lisp
(cmacs-calculator-defcalc simpleint (principal rate years)
  :category financial
  :title "Simple interest"
  :doc "Interest earned at RATE per year on PRINCIPAL for YEARS."
  :args ((principal "Principal amount")
         (rate "Annual interest rate as a fraction, e.g. 0.05")
         (years "Number of years"))
  :returns "Interest earned (not including the principal)"
  :examples (("simpleint(1000, 0.05, 3)" . "150"))
  (* principal rate years))
@end lisp

@strong{Never write a bare Emacs float literal in a @code{defmath} body.}
@code{defmath} passes Emacs floats through unconverted, and @code{math-mul} on
one yields a value that is not a Calc number, which breaks the next operation
--- the function then fails to fold and comes back unevaluated.  Use a Calc
fraction (@code{(/ 4 5)}) instead of @code{0.8}.  Relatedly, constants meant as
ordinary Lisp variables are spelled with a hyphen: @code{defmath} turns a bare
symbol such as @code{G} into the Calc variable @code{var-G}, but leaves any
symbol @emph{containing a hyphen} alone.

@subsection Tax API

Load @code{cmacs-calculator-tax-data} (not @code{cmacs-calculator-tax}) --- it
pulls in the math @emph{and} registers the tables.

@table @code
@item (cmacs-calculator-tax-federal INCOME STATUS &optional YEAR)
@itemx (cmacs-calculator-tax-state INCOME STATUS STATE &optional YEAR)
@itemx (cmacs-calculator-tax-fica WAGES STATUS &optional YEAR)
@itemx (cmacs-calculator-tax-self-employment NET-EARNINGS STATUS &optional YEAR)
@itemx (cmacs-calculator-tax-capital-gains GAIN INCOME STATUS ...)
Result plists carrying their own @code{:verified} and @code{:source}.
@item (cmacs-calculator-tax-bracket-liability TAXABLE-INCOME BRACKETS)
The marginal walk (the core).
@item (cmacs-calculator-tax-get JURISDICTION &optional YEAR)
A table, or nil.  With @var{YEAR}, that exact year --- deliberately @emph{not}
the nearest, since quietly answering with the wrong year's rates is the failure
mode the design exists to prevent.
@item (cmacs-calculator-tax-jurisdictions)
The 52 registered jurisdiction symbols.
@item (cmacs-calculator-tax-vintage JURISDICTION &optional YEAR)
@itemx (cmacs-calculator-tax-validate &optional JURISDICTION)
How old a table is; whether every table is well-formed (nil = all good).
@end table

Canonical filing statuses are @code{single}, @code{married-joint},
@code{married-separate} and @code{head-of-household}; common aliases
(@code{mfj}, @code{hoh}, @code{surviving-spouse}, @dots{}) are accepted, and
anything else signals rather than falling back to a status you did not ask for.

A bracket list is @code{((THRESHOLD . MARGINAL-RATE) ...)} ascending, first
threshold at 0, each rate applying to income @emph{above} its threshold up to
the next; the top band is unbounded.

@node Calculator Limitations
@section Limitations and Honest Caveats

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Domain rejections come back as a signal, not a number.}  Formulas with
bounded domains --- beta at or beyond 1, a radius inside the event horizon, a
refraction that is really total internal reflection --- call
@code{math-reject-arg}, the same idiom Calc's own @code{math-check-financial}
uses.  So @samp{lorentz(1.5)} signals rather than returning the complex number
@samp{(0., -0.894427191)}, which looks like a result and is not one.  An
argument is checked whenever it folds to a number, so @samp{lorentz(3/2)} and
@samp{lorentz(pi/2)} are caught too.
@item
@strong{The pendulum is small-angle.}  @code{pendulum} uses the small-angle
approximation; it is increasingly wrong as amplitude grows.
@item
@strong{Projectile motion is drag-free.}  Vacuum formulas --- no air resistance,
so real-world ranges are shorter.
@item
@strong{Gravity is standard, not local.}  Formulas use standard g
(9.80665 m/s@sup{2}), not your latitude's or altitude's actual value.
@item
@strong{Bohr energies are in joules}, not electronvolts --- the SI-always rule
applies even where a field conventionally uses eV.
@item
@strong{No unit tracking in the numeric families.}  Feeding grams or degrees
into an SI formula gives a wrong-scale answer silently.  Use the units functions
for dimensional work.
@item
@strong{Precision is a knob, not a guarantee of accuracy.}
@code{cmacs-calculator-precision} sets how many significant digits Calc computes
with.  It does not make an approximate formula exact.
@end itemize

@node Calculator Build
@section Build

@example
./configure ... --with-cmacs-calculator
@end example

The engine is Elisp over GNU Calc, which ships with Emacs, so the calculator has
@emph{no external dependencies}.  libregnum is @emph{optional}: with
@option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} the GPU chart tier is compiled in
(@code{HAVE_CMACS_CALCULATOR_CHART}); without it, configure notes that GPU
charts are disabled and @emph{the whole calculator still works} --- charting
falls back to the always-present SVG tier.  The MCP tools are registered when
@option{--with-cmacs-mcp} is also built.

@option{--calc} needs @emph{no} @code{standard_args[]} entry and no change to
@file{lisp/startup.el} (which errors on options it does not recognise), because
it is rewritten out of argv before Emacs parses it --- @option{--batch} and
@option{--eval} already exist and already work.  @samp{emacs -- --calc} still
passes @option{--calc} through as an ordinary argument.

Verify a build:

@example
$ ./src/emacs --calc "2/3*4"
2.66666666667
$ ./src/emacs -Q --batch --eval '(princ IS-CMACS-CALCULATOR)'
t
@end example

@noindent
If a change seems not to take, you have almost certainly hit the stale-Elisp
cache: Emacs prefers @file{.elc} / @file{.eln} over @file{.el}.  Either clean
them, or force source with @code{--eval '(setq load-prefer-newer t)'} --- a
stale @file{cmacs-calculator-tax-data.elc} will happily register a @emph{subset}
of the jurisdictions and everything will look like it merely works.
